:edition title='First Edition, revised (October 2026)'.
:p.This edition applies to VM/370plus Release 1 (kit build 5, 9 October
2026), an ESA/390 conversion of the VM/370 Community Edition V1 R1.2,
and to all subsequent builds until otherwise indicated in new editions.
:p.VM/370plus is a hobbyist project. It is not a product of, and this
manual is not a publication of, International Business Machines
Corporation. IBM, System/370, ESA/390, z/Architecture and VM/370 are
names of IBM products and architectures, used here only to identify the
systems this software runs on and descends from.
:p.VM/370 itself is in the public domain. The VM/370 Community Edition
is maintained by its community. Hercules is an open-source emulator.
Linux and Debian are trademarks of their respective owners. cREXX is an
open-source REXX implementation.
:p.The project repository holds the update decks, the build tools, the
issue register and the test runs from which every statement in this
manual can be checked. Comments on this publication may be addressed to
the project.
:eedition.
:toc.
:h1.Preface
:p.This manual introduces VM/370plus, tells you how to install it over a
VM/370 Community Edition system running under the Hercules emulator, and
describes how to use the two things it adds to VM/370&colon. virtual
machines larger than 16 MB with programs that run in 31-bit addressing
mode, and virtual machines that run the ESA/390 architecture, in which
Linux/390 and a complete Debian system run.
:p.It is written for the VM/370 user who already knows CP and CMS&colon.
how to log on, how to use the CP commands of the general user (class G)
and of the system operator (class A and B), how to edit and run an EXEC.
Where VM/370plus behaves as VM/370 does, this manual says nothing; where
it differs, it says how and why.
:h3.How this manual is organized
:ul.
:li.:hp2.Chapter 1, Introduction:ehp2., says what VM/370plus is and what
it is for.
:li.:hp2.Chapter 2, Differences from VM/370 Community Edition:ehp2.,
lists every externally visible change.
:li.:hp2.Chapter 3, Installing VM/370plus:ehp2., takes you from a fresh
Community Edition to a running VM/370plus system.
:li.:hp2.Chapter 4, Operating VM/370plus:ehp2., covers start-up,
shutdown and the rules that keep the system healthy.
:li.:hp2.Chapter 5, Running Linux/390:ehp2., describes ESA/390 virtual
machines, the Linux kernel, the Debian system and the network connection
to the PC.
:li.:hp2.Chapter 6, Service Machines:ehp2., describes CHATBOT, the spool
bridge to Linux services (LXCALL, PHONE, MQTT), RXPIP, and the service
machines that are planned.
:li.:hp2.Chapter 7, cREXX on VM/370plus:ehp2., describes the REXX
compiler and virtual machine built for and on VM/370plus, the languages
written in cREXX that run on CMS and on Linux, and Turbo CREXX.
:li.:hp2.Chapter 8, Compilers on CMS:ehp2., describes the language disk
and what is on it&colon. GCC 13 for C and C++, GnuCOBOL, COBOL with OO,
PL/I, Python, SQLite and SQLSERV, Pascal, APL, xBase, Smalltalk, ZIP and
TERSE.
:li.:hp2.Chapter 9, Documents and Printing:ehp2., describes TROFF,
BOOKIE (BookMaster markup), LaTeX and how PostScript reaches a printer.
:li.:hp2.Chapter 10, Restrictions and Known Problems:ehp2., lists what
does not work yet.
:li.:hp2.Appendix A:ehp2. summarizes the new and changed commands,
:hp2.Appendix B:ehp2. lists the files in the installation kit,
:hp2.Appendix C:ehp2. gives the ESA/390 deviations of the virtual
machine, :hp2.Appendix D:ehp2. lists all CP and CMS commands.
:eul.
:h3.Conventions
:p.Commands are shown in :xph.monospaced type:exph.. In command
descriptions, uppercase letters are typed as shown and lowercase words
stand for values you supply. Examples of terminal sessions are shown in
framed figures; lines you type are not marked separately because the
context makes clear which they are.
:h3.Summary of changes
:p.This edition is kept up to date as VM/370plus grows; each change is
listed here, newest first.
:ul.
:li.:hp2.13 October 2026.:ehp2. The spool bridge (Chapter 6)&colon. CMS
users call Linux services with :xph.LXCALL:exph., :xph.PHONE:exph.
(Asterisk) and :xph.MQTT:exph.; :xph.RXPIP:exph. brings REXX packages
from the rexxpository repository to CMS. Documents and printing (new
Chapter 9)&colon. :xph.TROFF:exph. (groff), :xph.BOOKIE:exph. (our
BookMaster-compatible formatter, which formats this Guide on
VM/370plus), :xph.LATEX:exph. (TeX Live 2023), Ghostscript, and
:xph.PSPRINT:exph. to a real printer. An ESA/390 guest reading the
virtual reader to its end no longer waits forever (I-276).
:li.:hp2.12 October 2026, evening (kit build 8).:ehp2. The language disk
VMLANG (MAINT 19F, :xph.LANG:exph.) carries every compiler and tool of
Chapter 8; AUTOLOG1 attaches it at IPL. New on CMS&colon. COBOL with OO
(:xph.COBX:exph.), xBase and Smalltalk as C programs, the SQLSERV
service machine (:xph.SQL:exph.), Stanford Pascal (:xph.PCPAS:exph.,
:xph.PCRUN:exph.), APL, :xph.ZIP:exph., :xph.UNZIP:exph.,
:xph.GZIP:exph., :xph.TERSE:exph. and :xph.UNTERSE:exph.. C programs see
real CMS file dates, :xph./tmp:exph. files and FILEDEF-style names.
Debian carries APL, COBOL, PL/I, xBase and Smalltalk with their tests.
:li.:hp2.12 October 2026.:ehp2. Compilers on CMS (new Chapter 8)&colon.
GCC 13 for C and C++ (:xph.CC:exph.), GnuCOBOL (:xph.COBOL:exph.), PL/I
(:xph.PLIC:exph., also on Debian), Python (:xph.PYTHON:exph.) and SQLite
(:xph.SQLITE:exph.); MODULEs run from EDF disks (I-271).
:li.:hp2.11 October 2026, evening.:ehp2. Larger minidisks&colon. CMS
under :xph.IPL 290:exph. reads, writes and formats IBM's Enhanced Disk
Format (4 KB blocks)&colon. :xph.FORMAT vaddr fm (BLKSIZE 4096:exph., a
full 3380 as one disk, files with more than 65,535 records.
:li.:hp2.11 October 2026, late afternoon.:ehp2. :xph.TDISK:exph. (a
temporary disk in one step); Linux IPLs from the reader again (I-265);
:xph.LPSW:exph./SVC fast paths for PSWs above 16 MB.
:li.:hp2.11 October 2026, afternoon.:ehp2. :xph.SHUTDOWN:exph. saves
warm start data again and the next IPL warm-starts by itself, spool
intact (I-267, I-268); each Linux user's address follows his CTC pair
(9.12a); ATTACH of a CTC next to another no longer stops CP (I-269).
:li.:hp2.11 October 2026, late morning.:ehp2. Two Debian systems at once
(M4b.3 complete); ESA/390 access registers saved per virtual machine.
:li.:hp2.11 October 2026, morning.:ehp2. Guest pages in real frames
above 16 MB (M4b.3)&colon. with :xph.MAINSIZE:exph. above 16, the frames
from 16 MB up to 160 MB hold virtual machine pages directly.
:li.:hp2.11 October 2026.:ehp2. GLOBALV.
:li.:hp2.10 October 2026, night.:ehp2. :xph.SET MACHINE:exph. and
:xph.QUERY MACHINE:exph.; FULIST (FL); EE, FSVIEW and the REXX
full-screen package under the EC-mode CMS; Appendix D, the CP and CMS
command tables.
:li.:hp2.10 October 2026, evening (kit build 6).:ehp2. :xph.IPL
LINUX:exph. and vmzipl&colon. Debian starts from its own disk; the
Debian disk is a 3390-9 (7 GB); no 64-CCW limit for Linux disk I/O;
mosquitto (MQTT) and Asterisk under Debian; :xph.QUERY SET:exph. shows
ESA; the CHATBOT service machine and :xph.CHAT:exph. (new Chapter 6);
RXBVM8 has TIME and DATE; the console banner reads VM/370plus; a fix for
memory corruption in Linux guests above 16 MB (location 80).
:li.:hp2.10 October 2026.:ehp2. Regina REXX and THE under Debian;
languages written in cREXX for CMS and Linux (BASIC, SNOBOL4, Pascal,
Lisp, Smalltalk, PL/M, Prolog, LOGO); Turbo CREXX on both; four more
minidisks for CMSUSER (196-199); the native build of the current cREXX
(M8.2); Python and GNU C under Debian; milestones brought up to date.
:li.:hp2.9 October 2026.:ehp2. First edition, for kit build 5.
:eul.
:p.:hp1.PC:ehp1. means the personal computer on which Hercules runs.
:hp1.Real:ehp1. storage and devices are those of the Hercules machine;
:hp1.virtual:ehp1. storage and devices are those CP gives a user.
:body.
:h1.Introduction
:p.VM/370 was written for System/370&colon. 24-bit addresses, 16 MB of
storage, S/370 channel I/O. VM/370plus is the VM/370 Community Edition
V1 R1.2 with its Control Program (CP) converted to the ESA/390
architecture. It runs on Hercules in :xph.ARCHMODE ESA/390:exph. and
gives its users&colon.
:ul.
:li.:hp2.Virtual machines up to 256 MB.:ehp2. CP builds ESA/390 segment
and page tables, runs in 31-bit addressing mode itself, and gives a
virtual machine as much storage as its directory entry allows.
:li.:hp2.31-bit programs under CMS.:ehp2. An EC-mode CMS, IPLed from
MAINT's 290 disk, lets programs obtain storage above 16 MB (the HIGHSTOR
facility) and run in 31-bit addressing mode, with a C compiler and
library for such programs.
:li.:hp2.ESA/390 virtual machines.:ehp2. :xph.SET ESA ON:exph. makes a
virtual machine an ESA/390 machine&colon. channel subsystem I/O (SSCH,
TSCH, MSCH, STSCH), the ESA/390 PSW and control registers, home and
primary address spaces, IEEE floating point. Linux/390 runs in it.
:li.:hp2.Linux and Debian.:ehp2. A Linux 4.0 kernel (31-bit, the last
Linux release that supports 31-bit machines) IPLs from the virtual card
reader and runs Debian 7 from a 3390 disk, with SSH access from the PC
over a channel-to-channel adapter.
:li.:hp2.cREXX.:ehp2. The cREXX REXX compiler, assembler and virtual
machine run on VM/370plus and are built on it.
:eul.
:p.Everything else is VM/370&colon. the same CP commands, the same
directory, the same CMS for 24-bit work, the same spooling, the same
operator. Programs that ran under the Community Edition run unchanged.
:h2.How VM/370plus is built
:p.VM/370plus is a set of UPDATE decks applied to the Community Edition
source, assembled on the system itself, and loaded into the CP nucleus
with the standard VMFLOAD procedure. The repository of the project holds
every deck, the generator that produces them, the build tools, the issue
register and the regression runs. Nothing in VM/370plus is a binary
patch.
:h2.Milestones
:p.The conversion was done in milestones, each closed by a run that
exercises it. Figure 1 shows where they stand.
