:figcap.The real machine
:efig.
:h2.Virtual machines
:ul.
:li.:hp2.Storage.:ehp2. A virtual machine may have up to 256 MB. The
directory's maximum (the second size on the USER statement) is checked
as before; the kit's directory allows 256 MB for CMSUSER and 64 MB for
MAINT.
:li.:hp2.Shared CMS.:ehp2. The CMS saved system is shared by page frame
rather than by segment&colon. with 1 MB segments, sharing whole segments
would expose private CMS storage to other users. The effect for the user
is none.
:li.:hp2.31-bit virtual machines.:ehp2. A virtual machine in EC mode may
run with PSW bit 32 on (31-bit addressing); CP translates its addresses
in 31 bits.
:li.:hp2.ESA/390 virtual machines.:ehp2. New command :xph.SET ESA
ON|OFF:exph.; see Chapter 5 and Appendix A.
:li.:hp2.More disk space for CMSUSER.:ehp2. Besides 191-195 on VM50U0,
CMSUSER has four more minidisks, 196-199, of 115 cylinders (about 52 MB)
each on VM50-8, in the cylinders the Community Edition leaves free
there. They come formatted, labeled CMS196 to CMS199&colon. :xph.ACCESS
196 H:exph. (197 I, 198 J, 199 K). A CMS minidisk on a 3350 holds at
most 65,535 blocks of 800 bytes, about 52 MB; larger minidisks come with
milestone M6.
:eul.
:h2.CP commands
:fig.
:table cols='3000 6638'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.Command
:c.Change
:ethd.
:row.
:c.:xph.SET ESA ON|OFF:exph.
:c.New (class G). ON makes the next IPL of a device an IPL of an ESA/390
machine; OFF returns to System/370 at once.
:row.
:c.:xph.SET MACHINE 370|XA|ESA:exph., :xph.QUERY MACHINE:exph.
:c.New (class G), the z/VM names. SET MACHINE ESA (or XA) is SET ESA ON,
SET MACHINE 370 is SET ESA OFF.
:row.
:c.:xph.IPL cuu:exph.
:c.A user who is running a shared named system (CMS) is IPLed with
CLEAR, so that no copy of the shared pages stays in the virtual machine.
:row.
:c.:xph.QUERY SET:exph.
:c.A fifth line shows :xph.ESA ON:exph., :xph.ESA PENDING:exph. (SET ESA
ON before the next IPL of a device) or :xph.ESA OFF:exph..
:row.
:c.:xph.IPL LINUX:exph.
:c.New. IPLs the Debian disk at virtual address 250 as an ESA/390
machine&colon. :xph.SET ESA ON:exph. and :xph.IPL 250:exph. in one
command. Works from a directory IPL statement too.
:row.
:c.:xph.IPL name:exph.
:c.Any other name always IPLs a System/370 machine; a pending :xph.SET
ESA ON:exph. waits for the next IPL of a device.
:row.
:c.:xph.DEFINE STORAGE:exph.
:c.Up to 256 MB. Leaves the named system the user was running.
:row.
:c.:xph.DISPLAY:exph., :xph.STORE:exph.
:c.Address storage above 16 MB.
:row.
:c.:xph.QUERY VIRTUAL STORAGE:exph.
:c.Shows five digits only (131072K appears as 31072K); :xph.DEFINE
STORAGE:exph. echoes the right value.
:etable.
:figcap.Changed CP commands
:efig.
:h2.CMS
:ul.
:li.The ordinary CMS (:xph.IPL CMS:exph., :xph.IPL 190:exph.) is
unchanged&colon. a System/370 BC-mode CMS with 16 MB of addressable
storage.
:li.An EC-mode CMS is on MAINT's 290 disk (:xph.IPL 290:exph.). With it,
the HIGHSTOR nucleus extension (loaded by the system profile) manages
storage from 16 MB to the end of the virtual machine; SVC 120
(GETMAIN/FREEMAIN RU) gives programs storage above the line.
:li.GCC380, the AMODE 31 build of the Community Edition's GCC, runs
above 16 MB; GCCLIB31 is a C library for programs that run in 31-bit
mode with their heap above 16 MB.
:li.Under the EC-mode CMS (:xph.IPL 290:exph.), minidisks can use IBM's
Enhanced Disk Format (EDF, 4 KB blocks) besides the 800-byte
format&colon. a disk up to the size of a full 3380 (518 MB), files with
any number of records. :xph.FORMAT vaddr fm (BLKSIZE 4096:exph. makes
one; ACCESS tells the formats apart by the label. The DMSEFS nucleus
extension does the work and is loaded by the system profile.
:li.New commands on MAINT 19D&colon. :xph.FULIST:exph. (:xph.FL:exph.),
a full-screen file list in the manner of FILELIST; :xph.WAKEUP:exph.;
:xph.CHAT:exph.; :xph.LINUX:exph.. :xph.GLOBALV:exph., global variables
as in z/VM, is loaded at IPL. Appendix A; Appendix D lists all CP and
CMS commands.
:li.Programs written for System/370 BC mode (MECAFF's EE and FSVIEW, the
REXX full-screen package) run under the EC-mode CMS too&colon. CMS turns
an SVC from BC mode into an EC call, and CP takes a BC-style system mask
(:xph.SSM =X'FF':exph.) in an EC-mode System/370 machine as the BC mask
it is.
:li.CPWATCH is not autologged&colon. it is an S/370 monitor that loops
under this CP. The two lines in AUTOLOG1's PROFILE EXEC are commented
out.
:eul.
:h1.Installing VM/370plus
:h2.What you need
:ul.
:li.A fresh, unmodified extraction of :xph.VM370CE_V1_R1_2.zip:exph.. Do
not install VM/370plus over a system you care about.
:li.Hercules 4.x (SDL Hyperion; tested with 4.7 and 4.9.1) or Hercules
3.13. Programs cross-compiled for 31-bit mode need Hercules 4.x, which
has the z900-level instructions in ESA/390 mode.
:li.A 3270 terminal emulator; the wc3270 in the Community Edition
package is sufficient.
:li.For Linux networking on Windows&colon. the CTCI-WIN package for SDL
Hercules (TunTap64.dll, with Npcap). On Linux or WSL2&colon. the tun
driver, and Hercules running as root or with hercifc installed setuid.
:li.The VM/370plus kit&colon. twelve zip files, each under 30 MB.
:eul.
:fig.
:table cols='1000 8638'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.Part
:c.Contents
:ethd.
:row.
:c.1
:c.:xph.vm370ce.conf:exph. (ESA/390), the shadow files of the system
volumes (CP nucleus, CMS, directory), README, tools
:row.
:c.2
:c.:xph.disks/vm50-4.cckd:exph.&colon. VM50-4 with its paging area
record corrected
:row.
:c.3
:c.the shadow file of the CMSUSER volume
:row.
:c.4
:c.:xph.vm370plus/linux/:exph.&colon. the Linux kernel and parameter
decks, the CMS files for MAINT 19D, kernel configuration and patches
:row.
:c.5
:c.the shadow file of VM50-3 (spool and paging)
:row.
:c.6-12
:c.the Debian disk, :xph.disks/lnx190.cckd:exph. (a 3390-9), in seven
pieces, with :xph.JOINDEB.CMD:exph. and :xph.joindeb.sh:exph. to join
them
:etable.
:figcap.The installation kit
:efig.
:h2.Installation steps
:ol.
:li.Unzip :xph.VM370CE_V1_R1_2.zip:exph. into a new directory.
:li.Unzip kit parts 1 to 15 over it, replacing files when asked.
:li.Join the Debian disk. In the Community Edition directory, at a
Windows command prompt&colon.
:eol.
:fig.
:xmp.
JOINDEB.CMD
Debian disk joined: disks\lnx190.cckd
:exmp.
:figcap.Joining the Debian disk (Windows)
:efig.
:p.On Linux or WSL2 use :xph.sh joindeb.sh:exph., which also checks the
result. The file :xph.disks/lnx190.sha256:exph. holds the checksum of
the joined file. Keep a copy of :xph.lnx190.cckd:exph.&colon. it is your
Linux system disk and Hercules writes to it.
:p.Join the language disk the same way (kit parts 14 and 15)&colon.
:xph.JOINLANG.CMD:exph. on Windows or :xph.sh joinlang.sh:exph. on Linux
and WSL2 gives :xph.disks/lang.cckd:exph., the 3380 at address 182 that
holds MAINT 19F (see Chapter 8).
:fig.
:xmp.
JOINLANG.CMD
Language disk joined: disks\lang.cckd
:exmp.
:figcap.Joining the language disk (Windows)
:efig.
:ol.
:li.If you want the Linux network, remove the :xph.#:exph. in front of
the line :xph.#0600.2 CTCI 10.1.1.2 10.1.1.1:exph. in
:xph.vm370ce.conf:exph..
:li.Start the system as you start the Community Edition&colon.
:xph.vm370ce.cmd:exph. on Windows, :xph.vm370ce.sh:exph. elsewhere.
:eol.
:h2.The first IPL
:p.The Community Edition's :xph.hercules.rc:exph. IPLs from 6A1 by
itself when Hercules starts. The first time, CP has no warm-start data
and asks for the start type; answer :xph./cold:exph.. When the console
shows :xph.DMKCPI966I Initialization complete:exph., the system is up.
:fig.
:xmp.
HHCCP041I SYSCONS interface active
VM/370plus Online
Start ((Warm|Force|COLD|CKPT) (DRain) (DIsable) (NOAUTOlo)) or (SHUTDOWN)
/cold
NOW 09:51:57 GMT FRIDAY 10/09/26
DMKCPI966I Initialization complete
:exmp.
:figcap.The first IPL of VM/370plus
:efig.
:attention.Do not type :xph.ipl 6a1:exph. again once CP has
initialized&colon. it resets the running system. Type it yourself only
if nothing has been IPLed (no DMKCPI messages at all).
:p.Then connect the 3270 emulator to :xph.localhost&colon.3270:exph. and
log on as usual.
:h2.Going back to the Community Edition
:p.Delete :xph.disks/shadows/*_1.shadow:exph. and restore
:xph.vm370ce.conf:exph. and :xph.disks/vm50-4.cckd:exph. from the
Community Edition zip.
:h1.Operating VM/370plus
:h2.Start-up and shutdown
:p.Operation is that of VM/370. The operator's console is 0009;
:xph.ENABLE ALL:exph. brings up the 3270 terminals. To stop the system,
the operator types :xph.shutdown:exph. on 0009 and then :xph.exit:exph.
at the Hercules console.
:attention.Always end with :xph.shutdown:exph. at the operator's console
before you leave Hercules. Killing Hercules leaves the shadow files
inconsistent. Never run two Hercules instances on the same disks.
:h2.Storage
:p.CP's own machine is 16 MB&colon. its nucleus, free storage, locked
and V=R pages, spool buffers and the pages of named systems stay below
16 MB. With :xph.MAINSIZE:exph. above 16 in :xph.vm370ce.conf:exph. (the
kit has 64), the frames from 16 MB up to 160 MB hold virtual machine
pages directly, and the storage above 160 MB, if any, is a paging store
that no channel addresses&colon. a page-out copies a frame there, a
page-in copies it back. Up to 2048 MB has been tested. Virtual machines
may be up to 256 MB.
:note.Because CP keeps its working frames in the low 16 MB, a 64 MB
Linux machine pages heavily, and while one runs, other users' work is
slower. Using frames above 16 MB directly (milestone M4b.3) is planned.
:h2.Rules that matter
:ul.
:li.A user who has IPLed the shared CMS may IPL another system; the
shared pages are dropped first (the IPL is done with CLEAR). The
shared-system models stay in storage until CP is IPLed again.
:li.CP emulates the System/370 interval timer from the TOD clock, since
ESA/390 has none; time slices end and virtual interval timers run.
:li.CPWATCH is not autologged (see Chapter 2).
:eul.
:h1.Running Linux/390
:p.This chapter describes ESA/390 virtual machines and Linux/390 in
them. Linux runs in two forms&colon. a small system in storage
(BusyBox), which needs no disk, and Debian 7 on a 3390 disk of its own.
:h2.ESA/390 virtual machines
:p.:xph.SET ESA ON:exph. makes a virtual machine an ESA/390 machine at
its next IPL of a device. Such a machine has&colon.
:ul.
:li.the ESA/390 PSW and control registers, primary, secondary and home
address spaces, and ESA/390 DAT, which CP shadows;
:li.the channel subsystem&colon. its devices are subchannels, I/O is
started with SSCH and completed with TSCH; channel programs may use
format-0 or format-1 CCWs and 31-bit IDAWs;
:li.IEEE binary floating point, the CPU timer, the clock comparator and
SIGP for its one processor.
:eul.
:p.:xph.IPL CMS:exph. (or any IPL by name) gives a System/370 machine
again; the pending :xph.SET ESA ON:exph. then applies to the next IPL of
a device. :xph.SET ESA OFF:exph. returns to System/370 at once. Appendix
C lists where the virtual machine deviates from the ESA/390
architecture.
:h2.What a Linux user needs
:fig.
:table cols='2000 2400 5238'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.Resource
:c.Virtual address
:c.How it is provided
:ethd.
:row.
:c.Storage
:c.64 MB
:c.:xph.DEFINE STORAGE 64M:exph., or 64M in the directory
:row.
:c.Console
:c.009 (3215)
:c.the terminal; Linux uses it in line mode
:row.
:c.Card reader
:c.00C
:c.the kernel and the parameters are IPLed from it
:row.
:c.Debian disk
:c.250 (3390)
:c.a real 3390, attached or dedicated to the user
:row.
:c.Network
:c.620 and 630 (CTCA)
:c.a CTC pair, attached or dedicated to the user
:etable.
:figcap.Resources of a Linux virtual machine
:efig.
:p.The operator attaches the devices&colon.
:fig.
:xmp.
attach 600 to maint as 620
attach 601 to maint as 630
attach 190 to maint as 250
:exmp.
:figcap.Attaching a Linux user's devices (operator)
:efig.
:p.Each device must be on its own virtual control unit (620 and 630, not
620 and 621). The second user's pair 602/603 goes to 622 and 632, which
gives that machine 10.1.2.2. For a permanent arrangement put
:xph.DEDICATE:exph. statements in the user's directory entry.
:h2.Starting Linux from its disk&colon. IPL LINUX
:p.The Debian disk carries its own kernel and IPL records, so Linux IPLs
straight from it. With 64 MB of storage and the devices attached (the
disk at 250), one CP command is enough, from CMS or from CP&colon.
:fig.
:xmp.
logon cmsuser
cp define storage 64m
cp ipl linux
Linux version 4.0.0+ ...
...
Debian GNU/Linux 7 vm370plus ttyS0
vm370plus login:
:exmp.
:figcap.Starting Linux from its disk
:efig.
:p.:xph.IPL LINUX:exph. is :xph.SET ESA ON:exph. followed by :xph.IPL
250:exph.. Options of the device IPL may follow (:xph.IPL LINUX
CLEAR:exph.). The kernel parameters, including the IP address, were
written to the disk with the kernel by :xph.vmzipl:exph. (below); the
reader route that follows remains for a different kernel or other
parameters.
:h2.Making a disk IPLable&colon. vmzipl
:p.:xph.vmzipl:exph. is the VM/370plus counterpart of :xph.zipl:exph.,
for the 31-bit Linux 4.0 kernel. It is installed on the Debian disk as
:xph./usr/local/sbin/vmzipl:exph.. Run it as root after changing the
kernel or its parameters&colon.
:fig.
:xmp.
vmzipl
vmzipl: /dev/dasda: kernel 1775 blocks in 151 runs, channel program 2560 CCWs in 5 blocks
vmzipl: parameters: no_removal_warning conmode=3215 condev=0x0009 vmip=10.1.1.2 vmpeer=10.1.1.1
vmzipl: IPL with  CP SET ESA ON  and  CP IPL <address of this disk>
:exmp.
:figcap.Running vmzipl
:efig.
:fig.
:table cols='2400 7238'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.Option
:c.Meaning
:ethd.
:row.
:c.:xph.-k file:exph.
:c.the kernel image (default :xph./boot/linux48.image:exph.)
:row.
:c.:xph.-p "parameters":exph.
:c.the kernel parameter line (default&colon. the running system's
:xph./proc/cmdline:exph.)
:row.
:c.:xph.-d device:exph.
:c.the disk to make IPLable (default :xph./dev/dasda:exph.)
:row.
:c.:xph.-r directory:exph.
:c.where that disk's file system is mounted, when it is not the root
:etable.
:figcap.vmzipl options
:efig.
:p.It writes :xph./boot/vmipl.bin:exph. (the kernel with the parameters)
and :xph./boot/vmipl.ccw:exph. (the channel program that reads it), then
records 1 and 2 of track 0 (IPL1 and IPL2) of the disk.
:h2.Starting Linux with the LINUX EXEC
:p.The :xph.LINUX EXEC:exph. is on MAINT's 19D disk, which every CMS
user accesses as the U disk. :xph.LINUX:exph. alone issues :xph.CP IPL
LINUX:exph.. :xph.LINUX READER:exph. takes the older route&colon. the
kernel and the IPL parameter files on 19D go through your reader. From
CMS, with 64 MB of storage and the devices attached&colon.
:fig.
:xmp.
logon cmsuser
cp define storage 64m
ipl cms
Ready;
linux reader
LINUX: punching the kernel and CMSUSER LXPARM to your reader ...
PUN FILE 0002  TO  CMSUSER  COPY 01 NOHOLD
PUN FILE 0003  TO  CMSUSER  COPY 01 NOHOLD
LINUX: IPL 00C as an ESA/390 machine
Linux version 4.0.0+ ...
...
root: the system on /dev/dasda1 -- switch_root
INIT: version 2.88 booting
...
Debian GNU/Linux 7 vm370plus ttyS0
vm370plus login:
:exmp.
:figcap.Starting Linux from CMS
:efig.
:p.The EXEC holds the files already in your reader, punches the two
pieces of the kernel into your own reader as one spool file and then
your parameter file, sets :xph.ESA ON:exph. and IPLs the reader. The
parameter file is :xph.userid LXPARM:exph. on the U disk, or
:xph.DEFAULT LXPARM:exph. if there is none; it carries your IP address.
Afterwards :xph.CP CHANGE RDR ALL NOHOLD:exph. releases your other
reader files.
:fig.
:table cols='3000 6638'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.File on MAINT 19D
:c.Contents
:ethd.
:row.
:c.:xph.LINUX48 KERNEL1:exph., :xph.KERNEL2:exph.
:c.the kernel with its initramfs, 91,696 card images in two files (a CMS
file holds at most 65,533 records)
:row.
:c.:xph.LINUX EXEC:exph.
:c.the EXEC described above
:row.
:c.:xph.DEFAULT LXPARM:exph.
:c.:xph.no_removal_warning conmode=3215 condev=0x0009:exph.
:row.
:c.:xph.userid LXPARM:exph.
:c.the same plus :xph.vmip=:exph. and :xph.vmpeer=:exph., the user's
address and the PC's end
:etable.
:figcap.Linux files on MAINT 19D
:efig.
:h2.Starting Linux by hand
:p.Without the EXEC, the operator spools the two decks from
:xph.vm370plus/linux/:exph. to the user's reader, and the user IPLs
it&colon.
:fig.
:xmp.
/cp spool 00c class *
devinit 00c vm370plus/linux/linux48.rdr ebcdic eof
/cp start 00c
devinit 00c vm370plus/linux/lxparm.rdr ebcdic eof
/cp start 00c
:exmp.
:figcap.Spooling the Linux decks (Hercules console)
:efig.
:fig.
:xmp.
logon maint cpcms noipl
cp define storage 64m
cp set esa on
cp ipl 00c
:exmp.
:figcap.IPL of Linux from the reader
:efig.
:p.The ID card of the two decks names MAINT; for another user change the
first card or spool the decks with :xph./cp spool 00c ...:exph. and
:xph.TRANSFER:exph.. IPL from the reader consumes the files&colon. spool
them again for the next IPL.
:h2.The Linux system
:h3.Boot sequence
:p.The kernel's initramfs (BusyBox) brings the 3390 online, creates a
partition and a file system on a new disk, groups the CTC pair as
:xph.ctc0:exph. and configures it from the IPL parameters (default
10.1.1.2, peer 10.1.1.1). If the disk holds a system
(:xph./sbin/init:exph.), it hands over to it with
:xph.switch_root:exph.; otherwise it stays in storage with the prompt
:xph.vm370plus&colon.~#:exph.. The parameter :xph.vmroot=ram:exph. (deck
:xph.lxparmram.rdr:exph.) keeps the in-storage system even when Debian
is on the disk, which is how you repair the disk.
:h3.Debian
:p.The disk is a 3390 model 9 (10,017 cylinders, about 7 GB). It holds
Debian 7.11 (wheezy) for s390&colon. sysvinit, udev, rsyslog, cron,
OpenSSH, apt and dpkg, root on ext2 (6.8 GB, about 6 GB free), a 128 MB
swap file. The disk carries its own kernel and IPL records, so :xph.IPL
LINUX:exph. starts it. Log in as :xph.root:exph. with password
:xph.vm370plus:exph., and change the password.
:p.Development tools are installed&colon. GNU C and C++ 4.6.3 with make,
Python 2.7.3 (:xph.python:exph.) and Python 3.2.3 (:xph.python3:exph.),
Regina REXX 3.6 (:xph.regina:exph. or :xph.rexx:exph., classic REXX as
on CMS) and THE 3.3, The Hessling Editor (:xph.the:exph., an XEDIT-like
editor; use it over SSH, since the 3215 console cannot show a full
screen). cREXX and the languages of Chapter 7 are in
:xph./usr/local/bin:exph..
:p.Servers are installed too. :hp2.mosquitto 1.6.15:ehp2., an MQTT
broker (:xph./usr/local/sbin/mosquitto:exph., configuration
:xph./etc/mosquitto/mosquitto.conf:exph., port 1883), with the clients
:xph.mosquitto_pub:exph. and :xph.mosquitto_sub:exph.; start it with
:xph.mosquitto -d -c /etc/mosquitto/mosquitto.conf:exph.. :hp2.Asterisk
1.8.13:ehp2., the telephone exchange, starts at boot and listens for SIP
on UDP port 5060; :xph.asterisk -rx "core show uptime":exph. talks to
it. Both are offered as experiments&colon. they work, at the speed of an
emulated mainframe.
:fig.
:xmp.
root@vm370plus:~# mosquitto -d -c /etc/mosquitto/mosquitto.conf
root@vm370plus:~# mosquitto_sub -t vm370plus/test -C 1 &amp.
root@vm370plus:~# mosquitto_pub -t vm370plus/test -m "hello MQTT on VM/370plus"
hello MQTT on VM/370plus
:exmp.
:figcap.MQTT on Debian
:efig.
:fig.
:xmp.
root@vm370plus:~# cat /etc/debian_version
7.11
root@vm370plus:~# df -h /
Filesystem      Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on
/dev/dasda1     6.8G  541M  5.9G   9% /
root@vm370plus:~# free
             total       used       free     shared    buffers     cached
Mem:         60512      21688      38824          0       2220      13792
:exmp.
:figcap.Debian on VM/370plus
:efig.
:h3.Languages on Debian
:p.Besides GNU C, Python and Regina, the C89 interpreters and
translators of the VM/370plus languages are in
:xph./usr/local/bin:exph.&colon. :xph.apl:exph. (APL), :xph.cobol:exph.
(COBOL with OO, mikebeer/cobol&colon. :xph.cobol prog.cob -o
prog.c:exph., then :xph.gcc -I/usr/local/share/cobol:exph.),
:xph.pli:exph. and :xph.plic:exph. (PL/I&colon. :xph.plic prog.pli:exph.
translates and compiles), :xph.xbase-c:exph. (xBase) and :xph.stc:exph.
(Smalltalk&colon. :xph.stc -L /usr/local/share/vmtests/smalltalk/lib
prog.st:exph.). :xph.sh /usr/local/share/vmtests/runall.sh:exph. runs
their test suites (APL 34, COBOL 20, PL/I 39, xBase 7, Smalltalk 22
tests).
:h3.The console
:p.The 3215 is a line device. Programs that use the full screen (vi, top
without :xph.-n1:exph.) draw poorly; use them only if you must. Ctrl-C
cannot be typed&colon. type :xph.^c:exph. and Enter instead (the 3215
driver turns :xph.^c:exph., :xph.^d:exph. and :xph.^z:exph. into the
control characters). When CP shows MORE..., press CLEAR or PA2.
:h3.Network and SSH
:p.Linux talks to the PC over a channel-to-channel adapter that Hercules
connects to a tun interface on the PC (CTCI). From the PC&colon.
:xph.ssh root@10.1.1.2:exph., or PuTTY to host 10.1.1.2, port 22. The
first login after a boot may take a minute while the SSH server gathers
randomness. Internet access from Linux needs routing and NAT on the PC;
:xph./etc/apt/sources.list:exph. points at archive.debian.org.
:h3.Stopping Linux
:p.:xph.poweroff:exph. (or :xph.halt:exph.) stops Debian; CP then shows
a disabled wait. :xph.CP LOGOFF:exph. releases the attached devices.
:xph.IPL CMS:exph. returns to CMS in the same session.
:h2.Several Linux users
:p.Each Linux user needs his own copy of the Debian disk and his own CTC
pair with a CTCI line in :xph.vm370ce.conf:exph.. The address follows
the pair&colon. real pair 06xx, attached as 62x and 63x (x = xx&colon.
600/601 as 620/630, 602/603 as 622/632) or at its real address, gives
the guest 10.1.n.2 with n = xx/2 + 1, and the CTCI line's PC end is
10.1.n.1. A boot script on the Debian disk (:xph.vm370plus-net:exph.)
sets it, so one disk image serves every user. The kit's example&colon.
MAINT on 600/601 and 190, address 10.1.1.2; CMSUSER on 602/603 and 191,
address 10.1.2.2.
:fig.
:xmp.
0600.2  CTCI    10.1.1.2 10.1.1.1
0602.2  CTCI    10.1.2.2 10.1.2.1
0190    3390    disks/lnx190.cckd
0191    3390    disks/lnx191.cckd
:exmp.
:figcap.Two Linux users in vm370ce.conf
:efig.
:p.Make :xph.lnx191.cckd:exph. a copy of the Debian disk. The disks are
identical&colon. the address comes from the IPL parameters, not from the
disk. Give Hercules enough storage for CP's paging store&colon.
:xph.MAINSIZE 256:exph. (the kit's value) for two Linux users, more for
more. With too little, CP fills its DASD paging space (:xph.DMKPGT400I
SYSTEM TEMP SPACE FULL:exph.) and the Linux machines fail.
:p.Two Debian systems run at the same time&colon. CP gives virtual
machine pages frames above 16 MB (with :xph.MAINSIZE 256:exph., up to
160 MB), so both working sets stay in storage. With :xph.CP IPL
LINUX:exph. from the disk, each system takes the IP address in the
disk's own parameters; give the second one its address with :xph.ip
addr:exph. (or IPL it from the reader with LINUX EXEC and its LXPARM).
:h2.Linux and CMS side by side
:p.Linux users and CMS users share the system as any two VM/370 users
do. Tested&colon. one user runs Debian and writes and reads files under
load, while another user works in CMS (LISTFILE, COPYFILE, TYPE, ERASE),
defines 64 MB, starts Linux with :xph.LINUX:exph., stops it with
:xph.poweroff:exph. and goes back to CMS with :xph.IPL CMS:exph.; the
Debian system's files are unchanged throughout.
:h1.Service Machines
:p.A service machine is a virtual machine that works for other
users&colon. it runs disconnected, takes requests and sends answers.
VM/370 has always had them (the operator, RSCS); VM/370plus adds new
ones. The first is CHATBOT. Others are planned (see the end of this
chapter).
:h2.CHATBOT
:p.CHATBOT is the persona chatbot of MCchat, written by the project
owner in cREXX and run by RXBVM8. It has ten personas, each in English
and German, and a knowledge base, all as CMS files; every answer is
authored text chosen the way the original bot chooses it. There is no
language model and no network.
:p.Users talk to it through the spool. :xph.CHAT:exph. punches the
question to CHATBOT's reader. CHATBOT reads its reader, runs the bot
once for all waiting questions, and punches each answer to the reader of
the user who sent the question (the spool file's origin, so nobody can
read another user's answers). The bot keeps each user's conversation
(persona, language, topic) in :xph.userid CBSESS:exph. on its 191 disk.
:fig.
:xmp.
chat hello there
Hello.
I'm Tchaika.
So - what brings you here today?
Ready;
chat /LIST
Personas (name/language): amicus/de amicus/en andron/de andron/en ...
chat /npc elena de
Oh - du bist es.
chat wie geht es dir?
Gut genug, um dich dasselbe zu fragen und es ernst zu meinen. Also - wie geht es dir wirklich?
chat /WHO
You are CMSUSER. You are talking to elena (de).
:exmp.
:figcap.Talking to CHATBOT from CMS
:efig.
:p.An answer takes a second or two&colon. CHATBOT waits with :xph.WAKEUP
(RDR:exph., which returns as soon as a file arrives in its reader, and
looks for a stop request every 30 seconds (INTERVAL in CHATBOT CONFIG).
:xph.CHAT:exph. alone starts a conversation that ends with an empty
line.
:fig.
:table cols='2600 7038'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.Command
:c.Meaning
:ethd.
:row.
:c.:xph./HELP:exph.
:c.the commands
:row.
:c.:xph./LIST:exph.
:c.the personas and languages installed
:row.
:c.:xph./NPC name [en|de]:exph.
:c.talk to another persona
:row.
:c.:xph./LANG en|de:exph.
:c.change the language
:row.
:c.:xph./RESET:exph.
:c.start the conversation again
:row.
:c.:xph./WHO:exph.
:c.who you are and whom you talk to
:row.
:c.:xph./DEBUG ON|OFF:exph.
:c.show how the bot chose its answer
:etable.
:figcap.CHATBOT commands
:efig.
:h2.Running CHATBOT
:p.The directory entry is in place&colon. 32 MB, :xph.OPTION
ECMODE:exph., :xph.IPL 290:exph., a 60-cylinder 191 on VM50U1 that holds
the bot, its data and the sessions. Start it from the operator
console&colon.
:fig.
:xmp.
autolog chatbot chatbot
AUTO LOGON   ***   CHATBOT  USERS = 005
:exmp.
:figcap.Starting CHATBOT
:efig.
:p.Its PROFILE EXEC starts the bot when the machine is disconnected, as
after AUTOLOG. An interactive logon (:xph.logon chatbot:exph.) gets CMS
instead, for maintenance&colon. :xph.EXEC CHATBOT (LOUD:exph. then runs
the bot on the console, :xph.EXEC CHATBOT (ONCE LOUD:exph. answers what
is waiting and stops. To stop the service, create :xph.CHATBOT STOP
A:exph. on its 191 (the loop ends at its next look) or :xph.FORCE
CHATBOT:exph.. To start it with the system, add :xph.AUTOLOG CHATBOT
CHATBOT:exph. to the start-up (AUTOLOG1's PROFILE EXEC).
:fig.
:table cols='3000 6638'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.CHATBOT CONFIG key
:c.Meaning
:ethd.
:row.
:c.:xph.DEFNPC:exph., :xph.DEFLANG:exph.
:c.persona and language for a new user (tchaika, en)
:row.
:c.:xph.WIDTH:exph.
:c.line width of the answers (72)
:row.
:c.:xph.CRISIS:exph.
:c.YES&colon. words from a persona's crisis list get its care text
:row.
:c.:xph.BOTCMD:exph.
:c.the command that runs the bot&colon. :xph.RXBVM8 CHATBOT -a CMS:exph.
:row.
:c.:xph.MAXBATCH:exph., :xph.INTERVAL:exph.
:c.questions per run of the bot (20), seconds between looks at the
reader (30)
:row.
:c.:xph.VAR name value:exph.
:c.variables the persona scripts may use
:etable.
:figcap.CHATBOT CONFIG
:efig.
:note.The upstream bot (build 1) runs unchanged. Its EXECs were adapted
to VM/370 and BREXX (no WAKEUP, no UPPER, EXECIO with variable records,
AUTOLOG instead of XAUTOLOG) and one bug was fixed; the list is in
:xph.vm370plus/chatbot/VM370PLUS.TXT:exph..
:h2.SQLSERV
:p.SQLSERV, the SQLite service machine, is in service&colon. see Chapter
8, SQLite. It is the model for the service machines below&colon. a
request is a file punched to the machine, the answer is punched back to
the sender.
:h2.The spool bridge&colon. LXCALL
:p.The spool bridge gives CMS users the services of the Debian guest
through the same protocol as SQLSERV and CHATBOT. :xph.vmspoold:exph., a
daemon in the Debian guest, reads its virtual reader, runs the requested
service and punches the answer back to the sender. The guest is MAINT
unless :xph.GLOBALV SELECT LXCALL SET SERVER userid:exph. names another.
:p.:xph.LXCALL service [arguments]:exph. sends a request and shows the
answer, which is also kept as :xph.LXCALL RESULT A:exph. (its first line
is :xph.VMSPOOL RC n:exph.); the return code of LXCALL is that of the
service, 404 if there is no such service. Options&colon. :xph.(FILE fn
ft fm:exph. sends a file as the input of the service, :xph.(SERVER
userid:exph., :xph.(WAIT n:exph. (seconds, default 900),
:xph.(NOTYPE:exph.. A service is any executable in
:xph./etc/vmspool.d:exph. of the guest; :xph.echo:exph. and
:xph.sys:exph. are there to try the bridge.
:fig.
:xmp.
lxcall sys
Linux vm370plus 4.0.0+ #8 Tue Oct 13 00:11:13 UTC 2026 s390 GNU/Linux
 01:26:49 up 10 min,  1 user,  load average: 0.93, 1.75, 1.15
Ready;
:exmp.
:figcap.A Linux service called from CMS
:efig.
:h2.PHONE and MQTT
:p.:xph.PHONE STATUS:exph., :xph.PHONE CHANNELS:exph., :xph.PHONE
PEERS:exph., :xph.PHONE CALL ext [context]:exph. and :xph.PHONE HANGUP
channel:exph. reach the Asterisk PBX in the Debian guest. :xph.MQTT PUB
topic message:exph. publishes a message (or, with :xph.(FILE fn
ft:exph., each line of a file) to the mosquitto broker; :xph.MQTT SUB
topic [n [secs]]:exph. waits for n messages. The broker can be another
one&colon. :xph.MQTT_HOST:exph. in :xph./etc/vmspool.d/mqtt.conf:exph..
:fig.
:xmp.
mqtt sub vm/test 1 20
vm/test hello from linux
Ready;
:exmp.
:figcap.A message from Linux arrives on CMS
:efig.
:h2.RXPIP&colon. REXX packages
:p.RXPIP is the package manager of rexxpository&colon. REXX routines are
published as packages, and a program names what it needs with
:xph./:hp1. REQUIRE pkg :ehp1./:exph. comments. The repository server
and the rxpip client run in the Debian guest; the bridge service
:xph.rxpip:exph. serves CMS users.
:p.:xph.RXPIP AVAIL:exph., :xph.RXPIP SEARCH term:exph. and :xph.RXPIP
SHOW name:exph. ask the repository. :xph.RXPIP BUILD fn:exph. takes
:xph.fn REXX:exph., installs the packages it requires and writes :xph.fn
EXEC A:exph., a standalone program with the packages merged in;
:xph.RXPIP RUN fn [args]:exph. builds and runs it. :xph.RXPIP GET
name:exph. fetches a package as :xph.file REXX A:exph..
:fig.
:xmp.
rxpip run demo
rxpip: mathutils 1.0.0 already installed
rxpip: greetutils 1.0.0 already installed
RXPIP: DEMO EXEC A written, 71 lines
HELLO, WORLD!!!
square of 7 is 49
Ready;
:exmp.
:figcap.A REXX program with its packages
:efig.
:h2.Planned service machines
:fig.
:table cols='2200 7438'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.Machine
:c.Purpose
:ethd.
:row.
:c.ASKSERV
:c.an AI gateway&colon. a Linux guest that relays questions to a
language model on the PC or in the cloud; CMS users ask it through the
spool, like CHAT
:row.
:c.MQ
:c.a queue manager on CMS
:row.
:c.Network pack
:c.optional&colon. a firewall guest (BORDER), an HTTP load balancer, and
a machine that starts more web guests under load
:row.
:c.SMAPI
:c.a subset of z/VM's systems management API
:etable.
:figcap.Planned service machines
:efig.
:h1.cREXX on VM/370plus
:p.cREXX is a REXX implementation built as a compiler (RXC), an
assembler (RXAS), a disassembler (RXDAS) and a virtual machine (RXBVM,
RXVM). It needs more than 16 MB to compile larger programs, which is
where VM/370plus came from.
:h2.The 2022 release, built on VM/370plus
:p.CMSUSER's disks hold the 2022 cREXX tree (F0041). In the EC-mode CMS
it is compiled by GCC380 with GCCLIB31 and runs in 31-bit mode with its
heap above 16 MB&colon.
:fig.
:xmp.
logon cmsuser
cp define storage 256m
cp link maint 290 290 rr
ipl 290
exec crxmk31
...
rxcn basic
rxasn basic
rxbvmn basic
0.1
Ready;
:exmp.
:figcap.Building and running cREXX on VM/370plus
:efig.
:p.:xph.CRXMK31 EXEC:exph. compiles the tree and links :xph.RXCN:exph.,
:xph.RXASN:exph., :xph.RXBVMN:exph. and :xph.RXDASN:exph. (about 90
seconds of CPU time). :xph.GCLB31 EXEC:exph. builds the GCCLIB31 library
itself. The output equals that of the cross-compiled build line for
line.
:h2.The 24-bit build
:p.The same tree also builds with GCC370 as a 24-bit program
(:xph.rxc:exph., :xph.rxas:exph., :xph.rxdas:exph., :xph.rxbvm:exph. on
CMSUSER's E disk); in the EC-mode CMS with :xph.global txtlib
gcclib:exph., :xph.rxbvm -l d ascommon:exph. and :xph.rxbvm -l d
asebcdic:exph. run their test sets.
:h2.Cross-compiled programs
:p.:xph.rxc31:exph., :xph.rxas31:exph. and :xph.rxbvm31:exph. on
CMSUSER's A disk were compiled on the PC with GCC 13 (:xph.-m31:exph.),
newlib and a small CMS runtime (:xph.vm370plus/m5f/:exph.). They need
Hercules 4.x.
:fig.
:xmp.
ipl 290
rxc31 basic
rxas31 basic
rxbvm31 basic
libctest a b
:exmp.
:figcap.Cross-compiled cREXX
:efig.
:h2.The current cREXX release
:p.The current release (1.0.0-beta.3) needs a C99 compiler; GCC380 is
GCC 3.2.3. It runs on VM/370plus cross-compiled with GCC 13
(:xph.RXC8:exph., :xph.RXAS8:exph., :xph.RXBVM8:exph., milestone M8.1).
:xph.RXC8:exph. needs :xph.LIBRARY RXBIN:exph. and :xph.RXCEXITS
RXBIN:exph. on the disk named by :xph.-l:exph.; source files are :xph.fn
CREXX:exph.&colon.
:fig.
:xmp.
rxc8 -l a -i a hello
rxas8 -l a hello
rxbvm8 -l a hello
Hello from current cREXX on VM/370plus
sum 1..10 = 55
Ready;
:exmp.
:figcap.The current cREXX on CMS
:efig.
:p.Milestone M8.2 builds the same release natively with GCC380 and
GCCLIB31 (:xph.RXC82:exph., :xph.RXAS82:exph., :xph.RXBVM82:exph.). The
three work for programs that call no library functions; the library
itself does not build natively yet (Chapter 9). Until it does, use the
M8.1 tools. Native cREXX on CMS runs in EBCDIC (IBM-1047) throughout, as
IBM REXX does.
:h2.Languages written in cREXX
:p.Several languages are implemented as cREXX programs. They are
compiled once on the PC and run unchanged on CMS (through
:xph.RXBVM8:exph.) and on Linux (through :xph.rxvm:exph.). Floating
point comes from :xph.rxfloat:exph., a cREXX version of the cREXX float
plugin, so no native plugin is needed.
:fig.
:table cols='1400 2100 2100 4038'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.Language
:c.CMS
:c.Linux
:c.Notes
:ethd.
:row.
:c.BASIC
:c.:xph.BASIC fn.bas:exph.
:c.:xph.basic fn.bas:exph.
:c.classic, MS and ANSI dialects (:xph.-MS:exph., :xph.-FB:exph.,
:xph.-ANSI:exph.)
:row.
:c.SNOBOL4
:c.:xph.SNOBOL fn.sno:exph.
:c.:xph.snobol fn.sno:exph.
:c.patterns, tables, arrays
:row.
:c.Pascal
:c.:xph.PASCAL fn:exph.
:c.:xph.pasc fn.pas:exph.
:c.compiles Pascal to cREXX, then runs it
:row.
:c.Lisp
:c.:xph.LISP fn.lisp:exph.
:c.:xph.lisp fn.lisp:exph.
:c.a Common Lisp subset; without a file, a read-eval-print loop
:row.
:c.Smalltalk
:c.:xph.SMALLTLK fn.st:exph.
:c.:xph.smalltalk fn.st:exph.
:c.Smalltalk-80 with GNU Smalltalk bracket syntax; class library in
:xph.*.ST:exph.
:row.
:c.PL/M
:c.:xph.PLM fn:exph.
:c.:xph.plmc fn.plm:exph.
:c.PL/M-80 compiled to cREXX; :xph.( COMPILE:exph. or :xph.-c:exph.
compiles only
:row.
:c.Prolog
:c.:xph.PROLOG:exph.
:c.:xph.prolog:exph.
:c.consult files with :xph.consult('family.pl')?:exph.
:row.
:c.LOGO
:c.:xph.LOGO fn.logo out:exph.
:c.:xph.logo fn.logo out.svg:exph.
:c.turtle graphics written as SVG
:etable.
:figcap.Languages in cREXX
:efig.
:p.On CMS the languages arrive as one reader deck (:xph.LANGS:exph.);
:xph.READCARD *:exph. puts the files on your A disk and :xph.LANGSUP
EXEC:exph. rebuilds the modules from their hex form. Use one of
CMSUSER's large disks as A for it (for example :xph.ACCESS 196 A:exph.).
CMS cuts a command argument to eight characters, so the EXECs pass file
names through the program stack&colon. type the names as usual.
:p.The languages need an EC-mode CMS with a large virtual machine&colon.
:xph.CP DEFINE STORAGE 256M:exph. and :xph.IPL 290:exph.. Their storage
comes from above 16 MB through HIGHSTOR; Smalltalk alone uses about 100
MB, so it is practical only when Hercules gives VM/370plus real storage
to match (the kit's :xph.MAINSIZE 256:exph.); with 16 MB of real storage
CP pages it to a crawl. Programs cross-built for CMS run on an 8 MB
stack.
:p.On Debian the languages are part of the cREXX bundle
(:xph.lxcrexx.tgz:exph.), unpacked under :xph./usr/local:exph.; the
examples are in :xph./usr/local/share/:exph.:hp1.language:ehp1..
:h2.Turbo CREXX
:p.Turbo CREXX is a small Turbo-Pascal-style front end&colon. a menu
that creates, edits, compiles and runs one cREXX program at a time. It
does not implement REXX; it calls the real cREXX tools.
:fig.
:table cols='1400 8238'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.Key
:c.Action
:ethd.
:row.
:c.N
:c.new program (from a template), then edit it
:row.
:c.O / E
:c.open an existing program; edit it
:row.
:c.C
:c.compile&colon. compiler and assembler, no run
:row.
:c.R
:c.compile and run
:row.
:c.L
:c.copy an example (hello, fibfact) to your disk or directory
:row.
:c.D
:c.show the versions of the tools
:row.
:c.Q
:c.quit
:etable.
:figcap.Turbo CREXX menu
:efig.
:p.:hp2.On Debian:ehp2., type :xph.turbocrexx:exph. (or
:xph.turbocrexx:exph. :hp1.file:ehp1.:xph..crexx:exph.). The editor is
:xph.$EDITOR:exph., else nano or vi. Underneath, :xph.crexx:exph.
:hp1.file:ehp1.:xph..crexx:exph. compiles and runs a program (:xph.crexx
-noexec:exph. only compiles)&colon. a small driver of VM/370plus for
:xph.rxc:exph., :xph.rxas:exph. and :xph.rxvm:exph., since the upstream
:xph.crexx:exph. driver is not yet built for s390.
:p.:hp2.On CMS:ehp2., type :xph.TURBO:exph. (or :xph.TURBO fn:exph.).
Programs are :xph.fn CREXX A:exph.; the editor is EDIT; compile and run
use :xph.RXC8:exph., :xph.RXAS8:exph. and :xph.RXBVM8:exph. with
:xph.LIBRARY RXBIN:exph. on A. Without the menu&colon. :xph.TURBO C
fn:exph., :xph.TURBO R fn:exph., :xph.TURBO E fn:exph.. The examples are
:xph.HELLO TCEXAMPL:exph. and :xph.FIBFACT TCEXAMPL:exph..
:fig.
:xmp.
turbo
  T U R B O   C R E X X   ---   VM/370plus CMS
  Current file: (none)
  N New    O Open    E Edit    C Compile    R Run
  L Examples         D Versions             Q Quit
Command:
:exmp.
:figcap.Turbo CREXX on CMS
:efig.
:h1.Compilers on CMS
:p.VM/370plus runs current compilers natively on CMS&colon. GCC 13 for C
and C++, GnuCOBOL 3.1.2 for COBOL, and a PL/I translator. They need the
EC-mode CMS (:xph.IPL 290:exph.), a large virtual machine (:xph.CP
DEFINE STORAGE 256M:exph.) and Hercules 4. Their headers and libraries
come from two tar files on an accessed disk, :xph.SYSROOT TAR:exph. (C)
and :xph.CXXROOT TAR:exph. (C++); programs see them as
:xph./usr/include:exph. and :xph./usr/lib:exph., read only.
:h2.The language disk&colon. LANG
:p.All the compilers and interpreters of this chapter are on one disk,
MAINT 19F (volume VMLANG, a 3380 at real address 182, attached to the
system by AUTOLOG1 at every IPL). :xph.LANG:exph. (on the Y disk) links
it read-only and accesses it as L; :xph.LANG fm:exph. uses another
letter. A file of the same name on one of your own disks comes first in
the search order, so erase old copies of these EXECs and MODULEs from
your A disk. :xph.TYPE LANG README L:exph. lists what is there.
:fig.
:xmp.
cp def stor 256m
ipl 290
lang
L (19F) R/O
LANG: language disk 19F is L -- HELP: LANG README
Ready;
:exmp.
:figcap.Accessing the language disk
:efig.
:h2.C and C++&colon. the CC EXEC
:p.:xph.CC fn:exph. compiles :xph.fn C:exph. with GCC 13 and links
:xph.fn MODULE:exph.; a file type of :xph.CPP:exph., :xph.CC:exph. or
:xph.CXX:exph. (or the option :xph.CPP:exph.) selects C++. The steps are
those of GCC on Linux&colon. the compiler proper (:xph.CC1 XIMAGE:exph.
or :xph.CC1PLUS XIMAGE:exph., run from above 16 MB by the :xph.XL:exph.
loader), the GNU assembler (:xph.AS:exph.) and linker (:xph.LD:exph.,
binutils 2.42), then :xph.ELF2CMS:exph., which turns the linked program
into a TEXT deck for :xph.LOAD:exph. and :xph.GENMOD:exph..
:fig.
:table cols='2400 7238'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.Option
:c.Effect
:ethd.
:row.
:c.:xph.OPT -O0:exph. ... :xph.-O2:exph.
:c.optimization (default :xph.-O2:exph.)
:row.
:c.:xph.CPP:exph.
:c.C++ for any file type
:row.
:c.:xph.NOLINK:exph.
:c.stop after :xph.fn O:exph.
:row.
:c.:xph.LIB name:exph.
:c.also link :xph./usr/lib/libname.a:exph. from the sysroot
:etable.
:figcap.CC EXEC options
:efig.
:fig.
:xmp.
cp define storage 256m
ipl 290
access 1b0 a
cc primes
primes 2000
PRIMES: 303 primes up to 2000, sum 277050, avg 914.356
Ready;
:exmp.
:figcap.Compiling a C program on CMS
:efig.
:p.Programs are compiled for 31-bit addressing, z900 instructions and
software floating point (IEEE, not hexadecimal), with newlib as the C
library&colon. the same as the programs cross-compiled on the PC
(Chapter 7). Inside a program, characters are ASCII; the run-time
converts to EBCDIC at the CMS file and terminal boundary. File names are
CMS file ids&colon. :xph.fopen("data.txt", "r"):exph. opens :xph.DATA
TXT:exph. on any accessed disk.
:p.C++ has the standard library of GCC 13 (strings, containers,
algorithms, iostreams) in the C locale. There are no exceptions&colon. a
:xph.throw:exph. ends the program with a message. Static destructors do
not run at exit.
:p.A compile of a small program takes about half a minute of CPU time.
Keep the work files on a large disk&colon. the compiler writes :xph.fn
S:exph., :xph.fn O:exph. and :xph.fn ELF:exph. beside the source and
erases them when it is done.
:h2.COBOL&colon. GnuCOBOL
:p.:xph.COBOL fn:exph. compiles :xph.fn COB:exph. to :xph.fn
MODULE:exph.&colon. :xph.COBC MODULE:exph. (GnuCOBOL 3.1.2) translates
the program to C, and the CC EXEC compiles and links it with libcob and
GMP from :xph.SYSROOT TAR:exph.. :xph.COBOL fn ( C:exph. stops after
:xph.fn C:exph.. Subprograms are linked statically (:xph.CALL:exph. of a
literal name).
:fig.
:xmp.
cobol hello
hello
      HELLO FROM GNUCOBOL ON VM/370PLUS
SUM 1..1000 = 000000000000500500
INTEREST =         89.51
Ready;
:exmp.
:figcap.COBOL on CMS
:efig.
:p.Files&colon. SEQUENTIAL, RELATIVE and SORT work files are binary CMS
files; LINE SEQUENTIAL files are text, converted between ASCII and
EBCDIC. :xph.SELECT f ASSIGN TO "rel.dat":exph. names :xph.REL DAT
A:exph.. INDEXED files and the SCREEN SECTION are not available. On
Debian, GnuCOBOL is a package of its own
(:xph.gnucobol-3.1.2-s390.tgz:exph.)&colon. :xph.cobc -x prog.cob:exph.
compiles with Debian's gcc.
:h2.COBOL with OO&colon. COBX
:p.:xph.COBX fn:exph. translates :xph.fn COB:exph. with :xph.COB2C
MODULE:exph. (mikebeer/cobol&colon. COBOL-85 with classes, methods and
:xph.INVOKE:exph., indexed files, SORT, free format and user functions)
into C89 and compiles it with the CC EXEC to :xph.fn MODULE:exph.;
:xph.COBX fn ( C:exph. stops after :xph.fn C:exph.. The run time is one
header, :xph.COBRT H:exph.. COPY books are :xph.name CPY:exph. files. On
Debian the same translator is :xph.cobol:exph.&colon. :xph.cobol
prog.cob -o prog.c:exph., then gcc.
:h2.PL/I
:p.The Community Edition's IBM PL/I (F) compiler is on the Y disk
(:xph.PLI fn:exph., then :xph.GLOBAL TXTLIB PLILIB:exph. and :xph.LOAD
fn (START:exph.). VM/370plus adds a PL/I-to-C translator
(mikebeer/pli)&colon. :xph.PLIC fn:exph. translates :xph.fn PLI:exph. to
C with :xph.PLI2C MODULE:exph. and compiles it with the CC EXEC to
:xph.fn MODULE:exph. (:xph.PLIC fn ( GCC380:exph. uses GCC380 instead).
SYSIN is the terminal and the console stack; a line of just
:xph./*:exph. is end of file. PL/I file names become CMS file ids&colon.
:xph.data/plitest_out.txt:exph. is :xph.PLITTOUT TXT:exph. (a part
longer than eight characters keeps its first and last four). On Debian,
:xph.plic fn.pli:exph. translates and compiles with gcc.
:h2.Python
:p.:xph.PYTHON fn [args]:exph. runs :xph.fn PY:exph. with the C89 Python
3 interpreter of mikebeer/python; :xph.PYTHON:exph. alone reads
statements from the terminal. NumPy, pandas and SciPy (the interpreter's
own versions), re, json, struct, cmath, complex numbers, classes,
generators and exceptions are built in. The interpreter (:xph.PY
XIMAGE:exph., 2.3 MB) runs above 16 MB through the XL loader. Integers
beyond ±999,999,999 are big integers. There are no sockets on CMS yet,
and modules of your own are :xph.name PY:exph. files on an accessed
disk.
:h2.Pascal
:p.:xph.PCPAS fn:exph. compiles :xph.fn PASCAL:exph. with the New
Stanford Pascal compiler (2026.10, Bernd Oppolzer) into P-code (:xph.fn
PCODE:exph.), and :xph.PCRUN fn:exph. runs it with the P-code
interpreter PCINT. Files are passed as :xph.DD_x=val:exph. arguments
(PCRUN), as the compiler documents. CE's own Pascal (:xph.PASCAL:exph.
on the Y disk) is unchanged.
:h2.APL
:p.:xph.APL fn.apl:exph. runs an APL script, :xph.APL:exph. alone is the
interactive session (type :xph.)OFF:exph. to leave). It is mikebeer/apl,
a C89 APL interpreter that writes primitives as words (:xph.rho:exph.,
:xph.iota:exph., ...), so it needs no APL keyboard; the module is
:xph.APLM:exph..
:h2.xBase and Smalltalk
:p.:xph.XBASE fn.prg:exph. runs a Clipper/dBASE-style program
(mikebeer/xbase) with the C89 interpreter :xph.XBASEC XIMAGE:exph.
(through the XL loader), or with the cREXX one when only :xph.XBASE
RXBIN:exph. is there. Tables are :xph.name DBF:exph. files with
:xph.DBT:exph. memos and :xph.XDX:exph. indexes. :xph.SMALLTLK
fn.st:exph. runs a Smalltalk-80 program (mikebeer/smalltalk, GNU
Smalltalk bracket syntax) with the C89 interpreter :xph.SMALLTC
MODULE:exph., or the cREXX :xph.SMALLTLK RXBIN:exph.; :xph.SMALLTLK
-i:exph. is the interactive prompt. Its class library is the
:xph.ST:exph. files :xph.KERNEL:exph., :xph.NUMBERS:exph.,
:xph.COLLECTI:exph. and so on. SmallIntegers are ±2^30 on CMS; larger
numbers are big integers. Both are on Debian as :xph.xbase-c:exph. and
:xph.stc:exph..
:h2.ZIP, UNZIP and GZIP
:p.:xph.ZIP zipfile files:exph. and :xph.UNZIP zipfile:exph. are
Info-ZIP zip 3.0 and unzip 6.0, so archives travel between CMS and the
PC&colon. text files are stored in ASCII, and CMS dates become the
archive dates. :xph.UNZIP -l:exph. lists, :xph.-t:exph. tests and
:xph.-o:exph. overwrites. A file extracts to its :xph.fn.ft:exph. name,
as text unless its file type is a binary one (ZIP, GZ, TAR, DB, O, ...).
:xph.GZIP fn.ft:exph. writes :xph.fn ft-GZ:exph. in gzip format, and
:xph.GZIP -d fn.ft-gz:exph. restores the file. Encryption is not
included. CE's MINIZIP and MINIUNZ remain on the Y disk.
:fig.
:xmp.
zip notes.zip report.txt data.csv
  adding: report.txt (deflated 61%)
  adding: data.csv (deflated 48%)
Ready;
unzip -l notes.zip
Archive:  notes.zip
...
:exmp.
:figcap.ZIP on CMS
:efig.
:p.:xph.UNTERSE fn.trs out.ft:exph. unpacks an IBM TERSE file (PACK or
SPACK, as AMATERSE or TRSMAIN write it on z/OS) into a CMS text file,
one record per line; :xph.UNTERSE -b:exph. keeps the bytes (variable
records get their 4-byte RDW), for binary data such as load modules or
dumps; :xph.UNTERSE -i:exph. shows the header (PACK/SPACK, RECFM,
LRECL). It is the Open Mainframe Project's tersedecompress, in C.
:xph.TERSE fn.ft out.trs:exph. goes the other way&colon. SPACK format,
RECFM F with the longest line as LRECL (:xph.-l n:exph. sets it,
:xph.-v:exph. makes V records); :xph.TERSE -b -l n:exph. packs binary
data. The encoder runs the decoder's own model and compresses as well as
IBM's SPACK.
:h2.File names in C programs
:p.Programs built with the CMS runtime (all those of this chapter) use
Unix file names and map them to CMS files&colon. :xph.fn.ft:exph. is
:xph.fn ft *:exph. for reading (any accessed disk) and :xph.fn ft
A:exph. for writing; :xph.fn.ft.fm:exph. and :xph.x/fn.ft:exph. give the
mode; :xph./tmp/fn.ft:exph. is a scratch file on A; a name without a dot
is :xph.FILE name:exph., as FILEDEF defaults it; parts longer than eight
characters are cut to eight. Text files are converted between ASCII and
EBCDIC (IBM-1047); file types such as :xph.O:exph., :xph.TEXT:exph.,
:xph.TAR:exph., :xph.ZIP:exph., :xph.GZ:exph., :xph.DB:exph.,
:xph.DBF:exph. and :xph.BIN:exph. are binary and passed through
unchanged. Absolute paths under :xph./usr:exph. are read from SYSROOT
TAR. File dates come from the CMS file status table.
:h2.SQLite
:p.:xph.SQLITE fn.db:exph. starts the sqlite3 shell (SQLite 3.46) on the
database :xph.fn DB:exph.; :xph.SQLITE fn.db:exph. :hp1.statement:ehp1.
runs one statement or dot command, for example :xph.SQLITE emp.db .read
load.sql:exph. for a script :xph.LOAD SQL:exph.. A database is one
binary CMS file; its rollback journal is :xph.fn DB-JOURN:exph. while a
transaction is open. One user at a time&colon. there is no locking
between virtual machines, so give each database to one user, or to the
SQLSERV service machine.
:p.:hp2.SQLSERV.:ehp2. A service machine that owns the databases and
runs requests for other users one at a time. :xph.SQLSERV:exph. waits
for reader files (:xph.WAKEUP:exph.); each request is punched to it, run
by :xph.SQLRUN:exph. against :xph.name DB:exph., and the answer is
punched back to the sender (its ORIGINID). From any user&colon. :xph.SQL
name:exph. :hp1.statement:ehp1. runs one statement, :xph.SQL name (FILE
fn ft fm:exph. a script, and :xph.SQL STOP:exph. ends the server;
:xph.(SERVER userid:exph. names another server than SQLSERV. Results are
rows with columns joined by :xph.|:exph., then :xph.SQLRUN&colon. n
statements, n errors:exph.. At the server console, :xph.STOP:exph. ends
it; :xph.SQLSERV (LOUD:exph. logs each request.
:fig.
:xmp.
sql emp select count(*) n, sum(salary) total from emp
n|total
3|25100.0
SQLRUN: 1 statements, 0 errors
Ready;
:exmp.
:figcap.A request to SQLSERV
:efig.
:fig.
:xmp.
sqlite emp.db .read sqltest.sql
dept   n  avg_sal  top
-----  -  -------  ------
Ops    2  7000.13  7100.0
R&amp.D    2  9000.25  9200.5
Ready;
:exmp.
:figcap.SQLite on CMS
:efig.
:h1.Documents and Printing
:p.VM/370plus formats documents in the Debian guest through the spool
bridge and gives the result back to CMS&colon. plain text as a CMS file,
PostScript as a CMS file that can be printed. Three formatters are
there&colon. troff, BookMaster markup and LaTeX.
:h2.TROFF
:p.:xph.TROFF fn [ft [fm]] (MACROS m:exph. formats a troff document
(file type TROFF by default) with groff and the ms, me, mm, man or mom
macros; the text comes back as :xph.fn DOCUMENT A:exph.. With
:xph.(PS:exph. the result is PostScript, :xph.fn PS A:exph..
:h2.BOOKIE&colon. BookMaster markup
:p.BookMaster is IBM's licensed program for GML markup. BOOKIE is our
own formatter for that markup&colon. :xph.BOOKIE fn [ft [fm]]:exph.
(file type SCRIPT by default) gives :xph.fn DOCUMENT A:exph.,
:xph.BOOKIE fn (PS:exph. gives :xph.fn PS A:exph.. Its style is the
style of this Guide&colon. a title page and an edition notice, a
contents page with page numbers, chapters on new pages, running heads
and feet, screens in framed boxes, tables and figures. This Guide is
itself available as :xph.GUIDE SCRIPT:exph. and is formatted on
VM/370plus with :xph.BOOKIE GUIDE (PS:exph..
:p.The tags are those of BookMaster&colon. :xph.&colon.userdoc:exph.,
:xph.&colon.prolog:exph. with :xph.&colon.title:exph.,
:xph.&colon.docnum:exph. and the title-page tags :xph.&colon.prodname
&colon.subtitle &colon.version &colon.based &colon.filenum:exph.,
:xph.&colon.frontm &colon.tipage &colon.edition &colon.toc &colon.body
&colon.appendix:exph., headings
:xph.&colon.h1:exph.-:xph.&colon.h6:exph., :xph.&colon.p &colon.note
&colon.attention:exph., lists :xph.&colon.ol &colon.ul &colon.sl
&colon.li &colon.dl &colon.dt &colon.dd:exph., :xph.&colon.xmp:exph. for
screens, :xph.&colon.fig &colon.figcap:exph., :xph.&colon.table
&colon.row &colon.c:exph., highlighting
:xph.&colon.hp1:exph.-:xph.&colon.hp3:exph., :xph.&colon.q:exph.,
:xph.&colon.xph:exph., symbols such as :xph.&amp.colon.:exph. and
:xph.&amp.amp.:exph., and the control words :xph..*:exph.,
:xph..sp:exph., :xph..br:exph., :xph..pa:exph., :xph..im:exph..
:fig.
:xmp.
:h1.Introduction
:p.This book is written in :hp2.BookMaster:ehp2. markup.
:ul.
:li.headings, paragraphs and highlighting;
:li.lists, screens, figures and tables.
:eul.
:exmp.
:figcap.BookMaster markup
:efig.
:h2.LaTeX
:p.:xph.LATEX fn [ft [fm]]:exph. (file type TEX by default) runs LaTeX
(pdfTeX, TeX Live 2023) twice, for the contents and references, and
dvips; the PostScript comes back as :xph.fn PS A:exph.. :xph.LATEX fn
(PDF:exph. runs pdfLaTeX and leaves the PDF in the Debian guest. The
packages are those of LaTeX itself, amsmath, graphics, tools, geometry
and url; a run takes about a minute.
:h2.Printing PostScript
:p.:xph.PSPRINT fn [ft [fm]]:exph. (file type PS by default) prints a
PostScript file. By default it goes to the virtual printer without page
headers; CP's printer 00E writes it on the PC, where
:xph.tools/psprint.py:exph. takes each PostScript job out unchanged and
hands it to the printer (lp, or a printer's port 9100) or makes a PDF.
With :xph.(BRIDGE [format]:exph. it goes through the spool bridge&colon.
Ghostscript in the Debian guest converts it to :xph.ps:exph.,
:xph.pdf:exph., :xph.ljet4:exph., :xph.pxlmono:exph. or
:xph.pxlcolor:exph. and sends it to the printer named in
:xph./etc/vmspool.d/print.conf:exph..
:fig.
:xmp.
bookie sample (ps
bookie: /var/spool/vmspool/out/operator/sample.ps, 12369 bytes, 5 page(s)
BOOKIE: SAMPLE PS A, 354 lines
Ready;
psprint sample
PRT  00E OUTPUT OF OPERATOR FILE  = 0041 RECDS= 000355 COPY= 01  A PRT
Ready;
:exmp.
:figcap.A document formatted and printed
:efig.
:p.Ghostscript itself (:xph.gs:exph., :xph.ps2pdf:exph.,
:xph.pdf2ps:exph.) is in the Debian guest for Linux users.
:h1.Restrictions and Known Problems
:fig.
:table cols='2400 7238'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.Area
:c.Restriction
:ethd.
:row.
:c.Real storage
:c.CP's frames are in the low 16 MB; storage above is a paging store.
Large guests page heavily.
:row.
:c.Linux disk I/O
:c.The initramfs still limits disk requests to 128 KB (from the time of
the 64-CCW limit, now gone); :xph.echo 760 >
/sys/block/dasda/queue/max_sectors_kb:exph. allows larger ones.
:row.
:c.Shared systems
:c.The models of a shared named system are not freed when the last user
leaves; they stay until CP is IPLed.
:row.
:c.CP dump
:c.DMKDMP cannot write its dump under ESA/390 (I-201); after an abend
the registers must be taken from Hercules.
:row.
:c.CMS
:c.The CMS nucleus is 24-bit; programs above 16 MB use HIGHSTOR and SVC
120.
:row.
:c.EDF disks
:c.Only under :xph.IPL 290:exph. (the 190 CMS sees an EDF disk as
unknown). A V-format file can only be extended at its end. Commands that
take record numbers (TYPE, COPYFILE FROM/FOR) reach records 1 to 65,535
directly; beyond that only in sequence. LISTFILE shows counts above
99,999 in thousands (:xph.1400K:exph.). The A-disk accessed at IPL must
be in 800-byte format. DISK DUMP/LOAD, TAPE, MOVEFILE and FULIST are not
yet checked on EDF disks.
:row.
:c.CPWATCH
:c.Loops under this CP; not autologged.
:row.
:c.QUERY VIRTUAL STORAGE
:c.Shows five digits.
:row.
:c.Multiprocessing
:c.One CPU only.
:row.
:c.cREXX, native
:c.The natively built current cREXX (M8.2) cannot build its library yet;
use RXC8, RXAS8 and RXBVM8 (M8.1).
:row.
:c.CMS file names
:c.Programs cross-compiled for CMS cut a file name longer than eight
characters to eight (Smalltalk's :xph.collections.st:exph. is
:xph.COLLECTI ST:exph.).
:row.
:c.C and C++ on CMS
:c.No C++ exceptions (a :xph.throw:exph. ends the program), no threads,
only the "C" locale. A compile needs :xph.CP DEFINE STORAGE 256M:exph.;
cc1plus takes one to two minutes of CPU.
:row.
:c.Language disk
:c.An old copy of an EXEC or MODULE of the same name on one of your own
disks comes first in the search order (PLIC, COBOL, APL ...); erase it.
:row.
:c.ZIP, GZIP
:c.No encryption. A file extracted from a .zip is text unless its file
type is binary. GZIP writes :xph.fn ft-GZ:exph. (CMS has no second dot).
:row.
:c.TERSE
:c.Files packed by TERSE read back with UNTERSE; that z/OS AMATERSE
reads them has not been tested yet.
:row.
:c.Smalltalk, xBase
:c.With a 32-bit :xph.long:exph., Smalltalk SmallIntegers are ±2^30
(larger ones are big integers); CMS has no directories.
:row.
:c.REXX EXECs
:c.An EXEC called with eight or more arguments ends CMS when the GCCLIB
segment is loaded (machines below about 15 MB); this is so in the
Community Edition too (I-277). Keep to seven.
:row.
:c.Spool bridge
:c.Requests and answers are 80-column cards&colon. a longer line of a
TeX or BookMaster source is cut.
:row.
:c.Linux
:c.Linux 4.0 31-bit is the last kernel with 31-bit support; Debian 7 is
the last Debian for s390 (31-bit).
:etable.
:figcap.Restrictions
:efig.
:appendix.
:h1.Command Summary
:h2.SET ESA
:fig.
:xmp.
SET ESA ON
SET ESA OFF
:exmp.
:figcap.SET ESA
:efig.
:p.Class G. :hp2.ON:ehp2.&colon. the next IPL of a device (not an IPL by
name) makes the virtual machine an ESA/390 machine; it stays pending
across :xph.IPL CMS:exph.. :hp2.OFF:ehp2.&colon. the machine is a
System/370 machine at once and nothing is pending. Any operand other
than OFF means ON.
:h2.SET MACHINE, QUERY MACHINE
:fig.
:xmp.
SET MACHINE 370|XA|ESA
QUERY MACHINE
:exmp.
:figcap.SET MACHINE
:efig.
:p.Class G; the names z/VM uses. :xph.SET MACHINE ESA:exph. and :xph.SET
MACHINE XA:exph. are :xph.SET ESA ON:exph., :xph.SET MACHINE 370:exph.
is :xph.SET ESA OFF:exph.. :xph.QUERY MACHINE:exph. answers :xph.MACHINE
370:exph., :xph.MACHINE ESA:exph., or :xph.MACHINE 370, ESA
PENDING:exph. while an ESA/390 IPL is pending. :xph.SET MACHINE Z:exph.
comes with milestone M9.
:fig.
:xmp.
cp query machine
MACHINE 370
cp set machine esa
cp q mach
MACHINE 370, ESA PENDING
:exmp.
:figcap.QUERY MACHINE
:efig.
:h2.IPL
:p.As in VM/370, with three changes&colon. :xph.IPL LINUX:exph. IPLs the
disk at 250 as an ESA/390 machine; any other IPL by name gives a
System/370 machine; an IPL of a device by a user who runs a shared named
system is done with CLEAR.
:h2.QUERY SET
:p.As in VM/370, with a fifth line&colon. :xph.ESA ON:exph. (an ESA/390
machine), :xph.ESA PENDING:exph. (:xph.SET ESA ON:exph. given, takes
effect at the next IPL of a device) or :xph.ESA OFF:exph..
:fig.
:xmp.
cp query set
MSG ON , WNG ON , EMSG TEXT, ACNT ON , RUN OFF
LINEDIT ON , TIMER ON  , ISAM OFF, ECMODE ON
ASSIST OFF           , PAGEX OFF, AUTOPOLL OFF
IMSG ON , SMSG OFF, AFFINITY NONE   , NOTRANS OFF
STBYPASS OFF , ESA PENDING
:exmp.
:figcap.QUERY SET after SET ESA ON
:efig.
:h2.CHAT EXEC
:fig.
:xmp.
CHAT [question] [(WAIT n BOT name LOUD]
:exmp.
:figcap.CHAT EXEC
:efig.
:p.On MAINT 19D. Sends the question to the CHATBOT service machine and
types the answer; without a question it asks repeatedly until an empty
line. :hp2.WAIT:ehp2. is the time to wait for the answer (default 60
seconds), :hp2.BOT:ehp2. another service machine, :hp2.LOUD:ehp2. shows
the steps. Keep your reader empty of other files while you use it.
Chapter 6.
:h2.WAKEUP
:fig.
:xmp.
WAKEUP [+hhmmss|+mmss|+ss] [(RDR QUIET]
:exmp.
:figcap.WAKEUP
:efig.
:p.On MAINT 19D (new; VM/370 had none). Waits until the interval has
passed (return code 2) or, with :hp2.RDR:ehp2., until a file arrives in
the virtual reader (return code 3); 24 means an invalid operand. A file
already in the reader does not end the wait, so look at the reader first
(:xph.QUERY READER:exph.). WAKEUP sets :xph.CP SET TIMER REAL:exph. so
that the interval timer runs while the machine waits. Service machines
use it instead of polling.
:fig.
:xmp.
wakeup +000005
Ready(00002);
wakeup +000500 (rdr
PUN FILE 0002 FROM CMSUSER  COPY 01 NOHOLD
Ready(00003);
:exmp.
:figcap.WAKEUP
:efig.
:h2.FULIST
:fig.
:xmp.
FULIST [fn [ft [fm]]]
FL [fn [ft [fm]]]
:exmp.
:figcap.FULIST
:efig.
:p.On MAINT 19D; a full-screen file list in the manner of IBM's
FILELIST, written in REXX with the REXX full-screen package; it runs
under :xph.IPL CMS:exph. and under the EC-mode CMS on 290. The default
is :xph.:hp1. :ehp1. A:exph.. Each line is an input field&colon. type a
command over the start of a line and press Enter. :xph./:exph. in the
command stands for the file (:xph.fn ft fm:exph.), :xph./n:exph.,
:xph./t:exph., :xph./m:exph. for its parts; without a :xph./:exph. the
file is appended. Abbreviations&colon. :hp2.E:ehp2. or :hp2.X:ehp2. edit
(EE), :hp2.B:ehp2. browse (FSVIEW), :hp2.D:ehp2. discard (ERASE),
:hp2.=:ehp2. repeats the last command. The status column shows
:xph.:hp1.:exph. or :xph.:ehp1.rc:exph. for each command run.
:fig.
:table cols='2400 7238'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.Key
:c.Action
:ethd.
:row.
:c.PF1
:c.HELP FULIST
:row.
:c.PF2
:c.refresh the list
:row.
:c.PF3, PF15
:c.quit
:row.
:c.PF4, PF5, PF6
:c.sort by type, date (newest first), size
:row.
:c.PF7, PF8
:c.page back, forward
:row.
:c.PF9
:c.FULIST of all files with the name at the cursor
:row.
:c.PF10
:c.sort by name
:row.
:c.PF11
:c.edit the file at the cursor
:row.
:c.PF12
:c.cursor to the command line and back
:etable.
:figcap.FULIST keys
:efig.
:p.On the command line (:xph.====>:exph.)&colon. QUIT, TOP, BOTTOM,
REFRESH, SORT NAME|TYPE|DATE|SIZE, FULIST pattern, or any CMS or CP
command.
:h2.TDISK
:fig.
:xmp.
TDISK [cyls] [fm]
:exmp.
:figcap.TDISK
:efig.
:p.On MAINT 19D (new). A temporary disk in one step&colon. CP defines it
(:xph.DEFINE T3350:exph.) at the first free address from D00 to D1F, CMS
formats it with the label TMPvaddr and, if a file mode is given,
accesses it. :hp2.cyls:ehp2. is 1 to 100 cylinders, default 10 (about 5
MB); without :hp2.fm:ehp2. the disk is formatted but not accessed. The
operands may come in either order. Return codes&colon. 24 invalid
operands, 28 the file mode is in use, 32 no free address or no TEMP
space; otherwise FORMAT's. The contents are lost at LOGOFF or DETACH.
:xph.HELP TDISK:exph. describes it.
:fig.
:xmp.
tdisk 5 t
DASD D00 DEFINED 0005 CYL
TDISK: disk D00 is ready as T, 5 cylinders, label TMPD00
Ready;
:exmp.
:figcap.TDISK
:efig.
:h2.FORMAT (BLKSIZE 4096)
:fig.
:xmp.
FORMAT vaddr fm [cyls] (BLKSIZE 4096|4K|800
:exmp.
:figcap.FORMAT
:efig.
:p.Under :xph.IPL 290:exph. (changed). :hp2.BLKSIZE 4096:ehp2. formats
the disk in IBM's Enhanced Disk Format&colon. 4096-byte blocks, the file
directory and the allocation map kept as files, records counted in
fullwords. Without :hp2.cyls:ehp2. the whole minidisk is used, whatever
its size (FORMAT's usual 800-byte limit, 121 cylinders on a 3380, does
not apply). 3350 and 3380 disks. FORMAT asks for confirmation and a
label as usual and then accesses the disk. :hp2.BLKSIZE 800:ehp2. is the
usual format. A disk written by :xph.tools/edf.py:exph. on the PC can be
accessed too, and the reverse.
:fig.
:xmp.
format 1b0 y (blksize 4096
DMSFOR603R FORMAT WILL ERASE ALL FILES ON DISK 'Y(1B0)'. DO YOU WISH TO CONTINUE? (YES|NO):
yes
DMSFOR605R ENTER DISK LABEL:
edf002
FORMATTING DISK 'Y'.
Ready;
q disk y
Label  CUU M  Stat  Cyl Type Blksize   Files  Blks Used-(%) Blks Left  Blk Total
EDF002 1B0 Y   R/W  885 3380 4096          0         10-00     132740     132750
Ready;
:exmp.
:figcap.FORMAT (BLKSIZE 4096)
:efig.
:h2.GLOBALV
:fig.
:xmp.
GLOBALV [SELECT group] SET|SETS|SETL|SETP name value
GLOBALV [SELECT group] PUT|PUTS|PUTL|PUTP name ...
GLOBALV [SELECT group] GET|STACK name ...
GLOBALV [SELECT group] LIST [name ...]|PURGE
GLOBALV GRPLIST
:exmp.
:figcap.GLOBALV
:efig.
:p.New; a subset of z/VM's GLOBALV. Variables live in groups (UNNAMED
unless SELECT names one). :hp2.SET:ehp2. keeps a value until the next
IPL of CMS, :hp2.SETS:ehp2. also in :xph.SESSION GLOBALV A:exph. (erased
at the next IPL), :hp2.SETL:ehp2. in :xph.LASTING GLOBALV A:exph. (read
again at every IPL), :hp2.SETP:ehp2. both. :hp2.PUT:ehp2. takes the
values of the calling EXEC's REXX variables, :hp2.GET:ehp2. sets them,
:hp2.STACK:ehp2. puts values on the console stack. A null value drops a
variable. Names up to 32 characters, values up to 255, 128 variables.
GLOBALV is a nucleus extension, loaded at IPL by the system profile.
:fig.
:xmp.
globalv select fulist setl editor EE
globalv select fulist list
FULIST   EDITOR = EE
:exmp.
:figcap.GLOBALV
:efig.
:p.FULIST takes its editor and browser from the group FULIST
(:xph.EDITOR:exph., :xph.BROWSER:exph.) when they are set.
:h2.LANG
:p.:xph.LANG [fm]:exph. links MAINT 19F read-only (if it is not linked)
and accesses it as :xph.fm:exph. (default L).
:h2.ZIP, UNZIP, GZIP
:p.:xph.ZIP zipfile files:exph., :xph.UNZIP [-l|-t|-o] zipfile
[files]:exph., :xph.GZIP [-d] fn.ft:exph. (Info-ZIP zip 3.0 and unzip
6.0; gzip format from zlib).
:h2.TERSE, UNTERSE
:p.:xph.TERSE [-v] [-l lrecl] in out:exph. packs a file in IBM TERSE
SPACK format (:xph.-b:exph. binary); :xph.UNTERSE [-b] in out:exph.
unpacks PACK and SPACK files, :xph.UNTERSE -i in:exph. shows the header.
:h2.SQL, SQLSERV
:p.:xph.SQL db statement:exph., :xph.SQL db (FILE fn ft fm:exph.,
:xph.SQL STOP:exph., :xph.(SERVER userid:exph.; :xph.SQLSERV
[(LOUD]:exph. runs the service machine.
:h2.LXCALL, PHONE, MQTT
:p.:xph.LXCALL service [args] (FILE fn ft fm | SERVER userid | WAIT n |
NOTYPE:exph.; :xph.PHONE STATUS|CHANNELS|PEERS|CALL ext [ctx]|HANGUP
channel:exph.; :xph.MQTT PUB topic message:exph., :xph.MQTT SUB topic [n
[secs]]:exph..
:h2.RXPIP
:p.:xph.RXPIP AVAIL:exph., :xph.SEARCH term:exph., :xph.SHOW name:exph.,
:xph.BUILD fn:exph., :xph.RUN fn [args]:exph., :xph.GET name:exph..
:h2.TROFF, BOOKIE, LATEX, PSPRINT
:p.:xph.TROFF fn [ft [fm]] (MACROS m PS:exph.; :xph.BOOKIE fn [ft [fm]]
(PS:exph.; :xph.LATEX fn [ft [fm]] (PDF:exph.; :xph.PSPRINT fn [ft [fm]]
(BRIDGE [format]:exph..
:h2.LINUX EXEC
:fig.
:xmp.
LINUX [DISK|READER]
:exmp.
:figcap.LINUX EXEC
:efig.
:p.On MAINT 19D. :hp2.DISK:ehp2. (the default) issues :xph.CP IPL
LINUX:exph.. :hp2.READER:ehp2. punches :xph.LINUX48 KERNEL1:exph.,
:xph.KERNEL2:exph. and :xph.userid LXPARM:exph. (or :xph.DEFAULT
LXPARM:exph.) to the user's reader, holding the other reader files, and
IPLs the reader as an ESA/390 machine. Needs 64 MB and, for Debian, the
devices listed in Chapter 5 under "What a Linux user needs".
:h1.Contents of the Kit
:fig.
:table cols='3200 6438'.
